Desired state|Terraform configuration declares infrastructure objects and their desired attributes.|Providers read remote objects and Terraform compares configuration, state and reality to plan actions. Configuration describes a goal, not a guaranteed sequence of shell commands. Review the proposed changes before allowing writes.|terraform plan
Initialization|Initialization prepares a working directory with backend configuration, provider plugins and referenced modules.|Run init after cloning or changing dependencies and backend settings. Backend migration and reconfiguration have different effects, so inspect prompts carefully. Initialization alone does not provision the declared resources.|terraform init
Planning|A plan previews actions needed to reconcile managed infrastructure with configuration.|Normal planning refreshes remote values, computes a dependency graph and shows creates, updates, replacements and deletes. A speculative plan can become stale. Save and review the plan used for a controlled apply.|terraform plan -out=tfplan
State mapping|Terraform state maps configuration resource addresses to remote object identities and tracked attributes.|Without the mapping, Terraform cannot reliably distinguish existing managed objects from objects it should create. State may contain secrets. Protect access, versioning and locking, and use supported state operations instead of hand-editing JSON.|terraform state list
Variables|Input variables parameterize a module's configuration.|Types and validation constrain accepted values; callers provide inputs through documented mechanisms with defined precedence. Defaults should be safe for the intended environment. A sensitive flag hides some display output but does not encrypt stored values.|terraform validate
Outputs|Outputs expose selected values from a module to callers or users.|Child-module outputs are referenced by the parent; root outputs can be displayed through the CLI. Sensitive outputs need access controls because machine-readable or raw output may reveal them. Avoid exporting entire resource objects unnecessarily.|terraform output
Providers|Providers implement the API-specific operations Terraform uses to manage resources and data sources.|Configuration selects provider settings and aliases; version constraints and lock files control plugin selection. Changing a provider version can alter schemas and behavior. Test upgrades and inspect resulting plans before applying.|terraform providers
Modules|A module groups related Terraform configuration behind inputs and outputs.|Reusable modules encapsulate a stable interface while root modules define deployable boundaries. Pin external module sources appropriately and avoid hiding every setting behind an oversized abstraction. A module does not automatically create a separate state or permission boundary.|
Lock files|The dependency lock file records selected provider versions and package checksums.|Commit it so collaborators and CI use consistent provider selections. Version constraints still define allowed upgrades; init -upgrade intentionally reselects within those constraints. The provider lock file does not pin remote module versions.|terraform providers lock
Remote state|A remote backend stores state outside the local working directory.|Central storage supports team access, durability and, depending on backend, locking. Enforce encryption, version recovery and least privilege. Remote state consumers may need access to more state data than the output interface suggests.|
State locking|State locking prevents cooperating Terraform operations from writing the same state concurrently.|Lock support and configuration depend on the backend. A lock error may represent an active operation, so identify its owner before intervention. Force-unlocking a live writer risks divergent state and conflicting remote operations.|
Sensitive values|Sensitive values are marked to reduce accidental exposure in human-facing output.|Marking a value sensitive does not remove it from state or saved plans. Control artifact storage, logs and backend permissions. Review provider behavior and avoid using secrets in names, tags or other inherently visible fields.|
Resource addresses|A resource address identifies a resource or instance in Terraform configuration and state.|Addresses include module paths and instance keys for count or for_each. Refactoring an address without a migration declaration can appear as destroy-and-create. Quote addresses containing brackets or keys correctly in shell commands.|terraform state list
Count indexing|count creates instances identified by numeric positions.|Removing an element from the middle of an input list can shift identities and trigger unintended replacements. for_each with stable keys is often more appropriate for named infrastructure. Changing the instance scheme needs an explicit state migration.|
Dependencies|Terraform dependencies determine ordering constraints in its operation graph.|References create implicit dependencies; depends_on adds an explicit relationship when data flow does not express it. Unnecessary broad dependencies can increase unknown values and serialization. Dependency order does not by itself mean an application is ready to serve.|
Data sources|Data sources read existing external information for use in configuration.|They do not usually take lifecycle ownership of the queried object. Values may be unknown until apply if they depend on planned changes. Make selection criteria stable so a broad lookup does not silently select a different image or network.|
Import|Import associates an existing remote object with a Terraform resource address.|The configuration must also describe the desired object. Import alone does not guarantee the next plan is empty or safe; defaults and mismatched attributes can trigger changes. Review ownership and the post-import plan before applying.|
Moved blocks|Moved blocks describe address changes during a configuration refactor.|Terraform uses them to preserve the mapping to an existing object instead of treating the old and new addresses as unrelated resources. Test migrations from supported previous versions and retain migration history when downstream users may upgrade later.|
Lifecycle protection|Lifecycle rules adjust planning behavior for managed resources.|prevent_destroy rejects plans that destroy a protected configured resource, but it is not a backup or universal deletion guard. Removing the resource block removes that configuration-based protection. Pair lifecycle settings with permissions and recovery procedures.|
Ignored changes|ignore_changes excludes selected attribute differences from normal update planning.|It can support shared ownership with an external controller, but also hides drift. Specify the narrowest attributes and document who owns them. Ignoring everything can leave Terraform showing stability while important security or capacity settings diverge.|
Replacement order|A replacement creates a new object and removes the old one because some attributes cannot change in place.|create_before_destroy can reduce downtime when names, quotas and dependencies allow overlap. It cannot make an incompatible database migration safe or bypass uniqueness constraints. Review the graph and temporary capacity requirements.|
Targeted applies|Targeted operations restrict Terraform's attention to selected resources and necessary dependencies.|They are useful for exceptional recovery but can leave the wider configuration unreconciled. Run a full plan afterward and resolve remaining drift. Routine targeted applies undermine confidence that the declared system matches reality.|
Provisioners|Provisioners run scripts or copy files as part of selected resource lifecycle events.|Terraform has limited understanding of what a script changed and how to undo it. Prefer images, cloud-init or configuration-management tools when possible. Script failure may leave partially configured resources requiring explicit recovery.|
Workspace boundaries|CLI workspaces maintain separate state instances for one working configuration.|They can separate similar deployments, but do not automatically isolate credentials, permissions or backend administration. Strong production boundaries may require separate roots, backends and identities. Always verify the selected workspace before planning.|terraform workspace show
Saved plans|A saved plan captures the intended actions and relevant values for a later apply.|Applying the file executes that planned decision rather than asking for a fresh interactive approval. Protect it as potentially sensitive and bind it to the reviewed commit and target. Discard stale plans after relevant changes.|terraform show tfplan
Drift policy|Drift is a difference between declared or recorded configuration and remote reality.|A team policy decides whether to revert an out-of-band change or adopt it into code. Refresh-only planning updates the proposed state view without requesting configuration reconciliation. Inspect evidence before overriding an emergency mitigation.|terraform plan -refresh-only
State splitting|State splitting moves ownership into smaller independently managed deployment boundaries.|Choose boundaries around lifecycle, team ownership and blast radius rather than arbitrary file size. Plan object-address migrations and dependency interfaces, and ensure no resource is simultaneously managed by two states. Maintain recovery copies during migration.|
Partial failure|An apply can complete some actions and then fail; it is not a global transaction.|Terraform records successful operations as it proceeds. Inspect state and real resources, fix the cause, and generate a new plan. Retrying blindly or assuming everything rolled back can compound the incident.|
Provider upgrades|Provider upgrades change the plugin implementation used to interpret and operate resources.|Review release notes, constraints and lock-file changes; test representative plans in a safe environment. Schema migrations and changed defaults can affect existing state. Do not combine a broad provider upgrade with unrelated high-risk infrastructure changes.|
Policy checks|Policy checks evaluate proposed infrastructure against organizational rules.|Combine syntax validation, plan inspection and checks for requirements such as encryption, tags or approved regions. A passing policy does not prove runtime correctness. Keep exceptions explicit and bind checks to the actual plan being applied.|terraform validate
